 times so rather use  fast iterations  and in each step evaluate our current  solution  intimate intermediate solution and then  make some appropriate changes  to it  and this also helps us to identify edge  cases and usually we do not see  all the implications of a task  at the first time we think about it  so we have a certain mental model and  during our process we need to update it  and to fill in missing information  and to do this we can for instance  test  with  at best realistic data and we not always  have this data  at hand maybe we can generate it  or we can ask experts or for instance  our future users  to supply us with them  and this helps as i already said a bit  of  finding edge cases and  finding missing data in our mental model  of the actual tasks  but  this will not always help us to find  out um  the  um  yeah  questioning our own thinking errors so  i highly encourage  everyone to  not only ask your internal rubber duck  but also talk to experts for instance in  the field in my case for instance i was  not working in computer vision before so  it really helped to get some additional  experience from people in my group which  had further knowledge on specific parts  for instance image processing methods  and to run down my ideas  and get another perspective on it  and actually  it helped also a great deal to have  curators in my group that are supposed  to use this application in the end  and they supplied me with some data of  their own so some images  in from their workflow  and yeah  based on that  um i needed still to evaluate with the  trade-offs  in mind  in the first place you wanted to have a  real-time  application  and  in the several steps of  building this application we had  different components  so imagine we would have an application  which which consists of four different  components and  we would have to  always have in mind that we have an  upper limit of our running time  and while building every  step  of this pipeline we needed to keep that  in mind to not exceed the napoli limit  okay so let me go back to the general  idea  so one of the problems  was  that  i wanted to have the  curators in the end  not  i didn't want to interrupt their  workflows so much because i think that's  highly critical if someone will  implement the solution  and  one of the things i wanted to give was  them the freedom to set the camera  somewhere else  so the angle doesn't matter so much  and also use different highlighter  colors not everyone will use for  instance yellow highlighting colors at  least not from what i saw and what  people told me as well  so i needed to keep that in mind  and the  actual pipeline we use looks like this  we have an input which consists of a  video stream  so basically a series of images  and  for the text extraction part we use the  original pdf as a reference  and extract images of every page from it  and then  match  our current image to this page  and do a feature extraction on both of  them to find the mapping between both  of these images  and as you already saw before you can  have a certain kind of skewedness you  don't have a perfect angle of incidence  of 90 degrees for instance  but  yeah there's a  lower limit to it of course and it also  depends on the feature extraction method  you're using  but  it gives the people more freedom  how they can use this in the end how  they can set up their camera for  instance use a smartphone camera at some  point  as well  and in the next step we need to detect  the highlights and then find the  corresponding bounding boxes  segment this  and then map them in back into the  reference image we took in the first  place  and extract the text from  and after that generating some output  they requested  some human readable formats and  i wanted to have some computer reward  formats as well for further downstream  applications  to come  okay so let me show you a few of the  details of this  problem  one of the challenges i already  